readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|In ''temperynge'' his colours, he lacked good size.
{{right|''Sir T. Elyot'', The Governour, iii. 19.}}}}

{{smaller block|The queen, sir, very oft importuned me
To ''temper'' poisons for her.
{{right|''Shak.'', Cymbeline, v. 5. 250.}}}}

'''2.''' To combine in due proportions; constitute; adjust; fit.

{{smaller block|But God hath ''tempered'' the body together: . . . that there should be no schism in the body, but that the members should have the same care one for another.
{{right|1 Cor. xii. 24, 25.}}}}

{{smaller block|Who of us can live content, as we are ''tempered'', without some hero to admire and worship?
{{right|''H. Bushnell'', Sermons for New Life, p. 57.}}}}

{{smaller block|Either this being should not have been made mortal, or mortal existence should have been ''tempered'' to his qualities. {{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, ix.}}}}

'''3.''' To moisten, mix, and work up into proper consistency; prepare by moistening, mixing, or kneading.

{{smaller block|After the clay has been allowed to "mellow, or ripen," in pits, under water, it is passed through the pug-mill and well kneaded or ''tempered''. {{right|''Ure'', Dict., III. 997.}}}}

{{smaller block|To ''temper'' clay means to mix it thoroughly, and prepare it for the use of the moulder, who must have it in a condition not too soft nor yet too hard, but in a suitable state of plasticity to be easily and solidly moulded into bricks.
{{right|''C. T. Davis'', Bricks and Tiles, p. 106.}}}}

'''4.''' To modify or qualify by blending: as, to ''temper'' indignation with pity.

{{smaller block|I shall ''temper'' so
Justice with mercy as may illustrate most
Them fully satisfied, and thee appease.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., x. 77.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|The young and happy are not ill pleased to ''temper'' their life with a transparent shadow.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, ix.}}}}

Hence — 5. To restrain; moderate; mitigate; soften; tone down the violence, severity, or harshness of; mollify; soothe; calm.

{{smaller block|ȝif thou tynez that toun, ''tempre'' thyn yre
As thy mersy may malte thy meke to spare.
{{right|''Alliterative Poems'' (ed. Morris), ii. 775.}}}}

{{smaller block|The waters whereof, temperatly drunken, did exceedingly ''temper'' the braine, and take away madnesse.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 332.}}}}

{{smaller block|"God ''tempers'' the wind," said Maria, "to the shorn lamb."
{{right|''Sterne'', Sentimental Journey (Maria).}}}}

{{smaller block|Gloomy canopies of stone, that ''temper'' the sunlight as it streams from the chapel windows.
{{right|''J. A. Symonds'', Italy and Greece, p. 173.}}}}

6. In ''music'', to tune or adjust the pitch of (the tones of an instrument of fixed intonation, like an organ or pianoforte), with reference to a selected principle of tuning. {{smaller|The term is also extended to the tones and intervals of the voice and of instruments of free intonation. See ''temperament''.}}

7†. To attune.

{{smaller block|He [Orpheus] wente hym to the howses of helle, and there he ''temprede'' hise blaundyssynge soonges by resownynge strenges.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Boëthius, iii. meter 12.}}}}

{{smaller block|Meanwhile the rural ditties were not mute,
''Temper'd'' to the oaten flute.
{{right|''Milton'', Lycidas, l. 33.}}}}

8†. To govern; control; regulate; train.

{{smaller block|He ''tempreth'' the tonge to-treuthe-ward and no tresore coueiteth.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xiv. 308.}}}}

{{smaller block|Cato . . . was so moche inflamed in the desire of lernynge that . . . he coulde nat ''tempre'' him selfe in redyng Greke bokes whyles the Senate was sittynge.
{{right|''Sir T. Elyot'', The Governour, iii. 24.}}}}

