'''temper''' (tem′pėr), ''n.'' [= It. ''tempera'', ''tempra'', temper, kind, sort, tempera; from the verb.] 1. Mixture or combination of different ingredients or qualities, especially in the way and the proportions best suited for some specific purpose: as, the ''temper'' of mortar.—2. Constitution; consistency; form; definite state or condition.

{{smaller block|Yorick was just bringing my father's hypothesis to some ''temper''.
{{right|''Sterne'', Tristram Shandy, ix. 32.}}}}

3†. Temperament.

{{smaller block|The exquisiteness of his [Christ's] bodily ''temper'' increased the exquisiteness of his torment.
{{right|''Fuller'', Pisgah Sight, I. 345. (''Trench.'')}}}}

4. Disposition of mind; frame of mind; inclination; humor; mood: as, a calm ''temper;'' a hasty ''temper;'' a sullen or a fretful ''temper''.

{{smaller block|A creature of a most perfect and divine ''temper''; one in whom the humours and elements are peaceably met, without emulation of precedency.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Cynthia's Revels, ii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Grave Henry hath succeeded him in all things, and is a gallant Gentleman, of a French Education and ''Temper''.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. iv. 15.}}}}

{{smaller block|Such as have a knowledge of the town may easily class themselves with ''tempers'' congenial to their own.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Various Clubs.}}}}

{{smaller block|It may readily be imagined how little such thwarting agrees with the old cavalier's fiery ''temper''.
{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 388.}}}}

5†. Calmness of mind; temperateness; moderation; self-restraint; tranquillity; good tem-

